body:
@luca-ctx 
Thanks for the merge and extra tests!
Appreciate your support!

I isolated the repeated-refresh part of this issue further.

There are two independent automatic scheduling paths:

1. filesystem and safety-reconciliation wakeups enqueue a periodic source refresh;
2. when the scheduler has no pending work, it can enqueue another periodic full refresh itself.

On an actively used host this becomes a loop because provider histories are watched recursively and change continuously.

I tested a local proof of concept where:

- source refresh runs only after an explicit/coordinated request;
- retries of an already requested failed refresh remain enabled;
- the daemon watches its configuration, but not provider histories;
- the daemon remains available for queries and relational hydration.

An isolated all-provider import completed successfully and published 646,412 documents. After the relational projection finished, a 30-second idle observation showed zero CPU ticks, zero I/O bytes, and no second refresh request. The same idle behavior was confirmed after deploying it against the existing production index.

This does not address all cold-import performance concerns in this issue, but it prevents continuously active provider files from turning the daemon into an effectively permanent full-import loop.

Fixed by #300, merged to main as 6964183ba351b958de0312d7f4030729e291ab09.

The public CLI contract is now:

- acknowledgement is sent only after the stable request ID and AdmissionPending state are durably persisted;
- equivalent manual All and periodic All demands share one physical executor, while non-equivalent work uses the bounded successor queue;
- provider discovery and capture do not block admission/status IPC;
- disconnect after durable admission has an explicit retain policy, with the stable ID and typed ambiguous-delivery recovery so callers can replay/observe the same request; definite pre-admission failures remain typed as not retained;
- restart recovers the durable queue into the owning coordinator before publishing the refresh endpoint.

Validation on exact candidate 2774c5fafe79c46cdedce8c8b92bda4d13b7310d included two independent final reviews with zero P0-P2 findings, Bazel CLI unit/search-refresh/setup-import suites, strict candidate Clippy, source-diff and formatting checks, Windows production compilation, and a bounded real-daemon reproduction. In that reproduction, a manual All submitted during a periodic cold All received durable acknowledgement in 12.7 ms, status remained responsive, the receipt reported one physical executor plus one coalesced logical demand, and a deliberate disconnect produced the documented retained-request receipt rather than a contradictory terminal transport failure.

Rechecked the new interrupted-background-refresh report against current `main` at `4e43bef2feab98db0206dd2bec14afb4056d4a72`. I have a synthetic engine-layer regression ready; it adds no production-code change.

The test:

- creates four synthetic route identities;
- durably records progress at 2/4, then injects an interruption before candidate publication;
- restarts against the same data root and request ID;
- proves recovery requeues at 0/4 with no current source and an empty `covered_route_ids` set; and
- proves the terminal receipt contains all four routes.

`git diff --check` passes, and the focused `publication_lifecycle_tests::restart_` filter passes on that SHA.

This is evidence that current engine recovery treats persisted progress as diagnostic rather than as restart coverage authority. It does **not** reproduce the full multi-provider, multi-gigabyte daemon process kill, so I am not treating the newer report as resolved from this test alone.

Would you prefer this regression as a focused test-only PR, or should I keep the evidence and any iteration on this closed issue?

## Exact code path, and why the two sides can never agree

**Readiness side** — `crates/ctx-semantic-index/src/query_index.rs`:

```rust
let semantic_documents = index.semantic_eligible_event_count().map_err(...)?;
let readiness = vector_store
    .source_backed_generation_pin_exact(index.generation_id(), semantic_documents)
```

and the comparison that produces the error:

```rust
let active_events = pinned.stats().active_events;
let eligible_events = projection.len();
if eligible_events > active_events {
    return Err(semantic_not_ready(
        "semantic_projection_event_mismatch",
        format!("Core generation {} selected {eligible_events} semantic events but the flat-F32 generation contains only {active_events}", ...),
    ));
}
```

**Projection side** — `crates/ctx-semantic-index/src/vector_store/source_projection.rs`:

```rust
let source_text = semantic_source_text(&document.text);
if semantic_core_content_is_control(&source_text) {
    retire.push(record.event_id.as_uuid());
    outcome.records_filtered = outcome.records_filtered.saturating_add(1);
    continue;
}
```

The predicate itself:

```rust
/// Control-message exclusion is applied only after complete normalized Core
/// content has crossed the generation pin.
pub fn semantic_core_content_is_control(text: &str) -> bool {
    ...
    user.starts_with("<environment_context>")
        || user.starts_with("<turn_aborted>")
        || user.starts_with("<subagent_notification>")
        || user.starts_with("Warning: The maximum number of unified exec processes")
}
```

The doc comment states the ordering explicitly: exclusion happens **after** the content has crossed the generation pin. `semantic_eligible_event_count()` is evaluated before that, so `active_events` is short by exactly `records_filtered` whenever any record matches — and `eligible_events > active_events` then fires permanently.

There is a second filter site immediately above it (`builder.build_document(record)?` returning `None` also increments `records_filtered`), so the shortfall is not limited to control messages.

## Why this hits Codex users in particular

Every one of those four markers is a Codex session artifact. On this machine `~/.codex/sessions` is the larger provider, and the filtered counts tracked it exactly:

| corpus | selected | projected | filtered | identity |
|--------|---------:|----------:|---------:|----------|
| 103,010 events | 3494 | 3210 | 284 | 3210 + 284 = 3494 |
| 36,241 events  | 1304 | 1240 |  64 | 1240 +  64 = 1304 |

Any user whose history includes at least one `<environment_context>` turn — i.e. essentially every Codex user — should be permanently unable to use `--backend semantic`.

## Suggested invariant

The acknowledgement record already persists both numbers, so the information needed is present:

```json
{ "semantic_documents": 3494, "projected_documents": 3210, ... }
```

Gating on

```
active_events + records_filtered == semantic_documents
```

would keep the integrity check meaningful. Comparing against `projected_documents` alone would also clear the error, but it would no longer detect a genuinely truncated projection, which appears to be the point of the exact pin.

Pre-filtering on the Core side is presumably not an option given the design note that exclusion requires complete normalized content — hence the suggestion to carry the filtered count across the pin instead.

(Testing note: I could not validate a patch locally — #633 reports the workspace clippy/test gates failing on non-Linux hosts, and this machine is Windows.)

Follow-up: on Windows there is currently no user-side workaround for this, because the escape hatch the error message suggests cannot reach the process that needs it.

**1. The suggested environment variables never reach the daemon.**

Only the daemon produces semantic projections. ctx launches its daemon from a scheduled task whose action clears the environment and rebuilds it from a fixed 11-name allowlist. Decoded from `~/.ctx/daemon/windows-task.xml`:

```powershell
$p.EnvironmentVariables.Clear();
$p.EnvironmentVariables['APPDATA']='C:\Users\<u>\AppData\Roaming';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['HOME']='C:\Users\<u>';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['HOMEDRIVE']='C:';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['HOMEPATH']='\Users\<u>';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['LOCALAPPDATA']='C:\Users\<u>\AppData\Local';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['PATH']='C:\Windows\System32;C:\Windows';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['SystemRoot']='C:\WINDOWS';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['TEMP']='C:\Users\<u>\AppData\Local\Temp';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['TMP']='C:\Users\<u>\AppData\Local\Temp';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['USERPROFILE']='C:\Users\<u>';
$p.EnvironmentVariables['WINDIR']='C:\WINDOWS';
$p.Arguments='--data-root C:\Users\<u>\.ctx daemon run --format=json';
```

`CTX_ONNXRUNTIME_DYLIB`, `ORT_DYLIB_PATH`, `CTX_ONNXRUNTIME_DIR`, `CTX_ONNXRUNTIME_CACHE_DIR` and `CTX_RUNTIME_DIR` are all `CTX_*`/`ORT_*` names, none of which is on that allowlist. The daemon therefore reports

```
no ONNX Runtime dynamic library candidates were found for windows-x64;
set an absolute path with CTX_ONNXRUNTIME_DYLIB, ORT_DYLIB_PATH, ...
```

advising a fix that is structurally impossible for it to observe.

**2. Setting them on a foreground `ctx upgrade` does not help either** — the extract still dies on the unbound `$Destination`. Tried `CTX_RUNTIME_DIR=<data-root>\runtime` and `CTX_ONNXRUNTIME_DIR=<data-root>\runtime\onnxruntime`.

**3. Hand-placing the runtime is not trusted.** I extracted the downloaded `.artifact` to the documented location using ctx's own generated script:

```
$HOME/.ctx/runtime/onnxruntime/1.27.0/windows-x64/
    lib/onnxruntime.dll, lib/msvcp140.dll, ...
    LICENSE, VERSION_NUMBER, GIT_COMMIT_ID, ...
```

(also tried with the DLLs additionally at the platform root, matching the 0.25/1.0 layout). Discovery still reports no candidates — presumably because `ctx-runtime-install.json` is absent, and it is verified against the signed release metadata, so it cannot be written by hand.

**One more observable that supports the diagnosis:** ctx creates `runtime/onnxruntime/1.27.0/` and stops there. The `<platform>` leaf is never created. That is exactly where the destination path would gain its last component, which fits `$Destination` being null at the point the extract script is invoked.
